{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cek7KgtNWfxcr4A77qzt7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a self-explanatory pair. Commit `a39de71` explicitly changed `@Configuration` to `@EnableWebSecurity`, which caused the MIME type error (this commit's message literally documents the introduced bug), and `7f9126f` reverts exactly that change back to `@Configuration`.\n\n## Bug-Introducing Commits\nBIC: a39de719e930f450c488dabd21f66f7221e5f80d\n\n## Reasoning\n(1) I traced the file history of `SecurityConfig.java` using `git log --follow`, which showed the fix commit `7f9126f` immediately preceded by `a39de71`. I then examined the diff of `a39de71`.\n\n(2) That commit swapped `@Configuration` for `@EnableWebSecurity` on the `SecurityConfig` class (and added the corresponding import). `@EnableWebSecurity` triggers Spring Security's default web security configuration alongside the custom `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter`, which in this setup caused static resources (like JS files) to be served/handled incorrectly, resulting in the browser receiving `text/html` content instead of the expected JavaScript MIME type for `hello.js`, triggering strict MIME-type checking errors.\n\n(3) The evidence is direct and unambiguous: the introducing commit's own message states \"introduce error: Refused to execute script from 'http://localhost:8080/js/hello.js' because its MIME type ('text/html') is not executable, and strict MIME type checking is enabled.\" — an exact match to the bug being fixed. The fix commit `7f9126f` reverts precisely this one-line annotation change (`@EnableWebSecurity` → `@Configuration`), confirming a clean introduce/fix pair with no ambiguity."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":804,"cache_read_input_tokens":12526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":804},"output_tokens":1,